✅ SQL (Relational Databases):
Structure: Table-based (rows & columns)

Schema: Fixed schema (predefined structure)

ACID Compliance: Strong (ensures data integrity)

Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server

Best For: Structured data, complex queries, multi-row transactions




✅ NoSQL (Non-Relational Databases):
Structure: Document, Key-Value, Column, or Graph based

Schema: Dynamic schema (flexible structure)

CAP Theorem: Prioritizes availability and partition tolerance

Examples: MongoDB, Cassandra, Redis, Neo4j, DynamoDB

Best For: Unstructured/ semi-structured data, high scalability, large data volumes



⚖️ Summary:
| Feature            | SQL                    | NoSQL                         |
| ------------------ | ---------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| **Data Format**    | Tables                 | JSON, key-value, graph, etc.  |
| **Schema**         | Rigid                  | Flexible                      |
| **Scalability**    | Vertical               | Horizontal                    |
| **Transactions**   | Strong (ACID)          | Weaker (eventual consistency) |
| **Query Language** | SQL                    | Varies (Mongo Query, etc.)    |
| **Use Case**       | Financial systems, ERP | Big data, real-time analytics |


